Subject: webgpu: serve no adapter when the resolved identity measured none

Patch 0102 makes a host with no GPU compose a hardware GPU identity, which
turns a latent catalogue hole into a reachable one. Two of
linux-vulkan-nvidia-adf287b8f0ee's four measured members -- the RTX 3090 and
the RTX PRO 4000 Blackwell -- returned no WebGPU adapter on the machines they
were captured from. `capability_cluster.webgpu.uniform` is false on that anchor
for exactly this reason, and Compose merges no webgpu fragment for those
members, so navigator.gpu was left the host's own.

On a host with a real NVIDIA card that is the measurement reproduced. On a host
with no GPU it is not. Measured through the shipped 152.0.7977.83 macOS
artifact under --use-gl=angle --use-angle=swiftshader,
navigator.gpu.requestAdapter() returns an adapter whose info reads
{vendor: "google", architecture: "swiftshader"}, with 36 limits and 21
features. So those two identities would present a GeForce renderer string on
WebGL beside a self-identifying software adapter on WebGPU -- one property
read, no allocation, no timing. That is cheaper to detect than anything in the
limit tables.

Three ways out, and only one of them asserts nothing unmeasured.

Claiming the sibling Ada members' nvidia/lovelace adapter is refused for the
reason the anchor already gives: this silicon is Ampere and Blackwell, so
lovelace would be a falsifiable claim about a generation these boards are not.

Dropping the two identities from the draw on a software host would cut that
anchor's pool from eleven to nine, trading a one-property tell for reduced
per-seed entropy. A smaller pool is the cross-seed linking defect this series
has been closing, so that trade is backwards.

Serving what the member measured -- no adapter -- is faithful replay, which is
the model the corpus is built on. It reproduces the capture rather than
improving on it. It is also the unremarkable answer on that host: stock Chrome
151 on a GPU-less headless Linux box returns no adapter either, measured, so
null is what the population does there.

The lever is admission rather than suppression, which is why this patch is
small. Patch 0056's CanUseProfileSoftwareWebGPU is what opts a launch into the
SwiftShader-only Dawn provider, and that opt-in is the whole reason
navigator.gpu answers at all on a GPU-less host. Requiring the profile to
describe an adapter before opting in means a profile that describes none simply
does not opt in, and Dawn then enumerates as stock does and finds nothing. No
new suppression path, no second mechanism to keep in step with the first.

ProfileDeclaresWebGPUAdapter() lives in the policy header beside the predicate
that consumes it, because there are two call sites -- the WebGPU decoder and
the GL texture shared-image backing factory -- and two copies of a predicate
about the same profile would eventually disagree. Any one populated field
counts: Compose merges a member's WebGPU fragment whole or not at all, so for a
composed profile this is all-or-nothing, and the any-field form only matters
for a hand-written --apostate-profile, where refusing on a partial fragment
would silently drop a claim the user did make. A partial claim is served as far
as it goes and an absent one is not invented.

The field defaults false, so a caller that has not considered the question gets
stock behaviour rather than an adapter by omission. That default is what makes
the existing policy tests fail until they state their intent, which is the
point of a required field, and the two that were about other things now set it.
A third case is added for the behaviour itself.

Nothing changes for any identity that measured an adapter, which is all fifteen
Windows identities, all twelve Apple ones and nine of the eleven Vulkan ones.
Their vendor, architecture and full 36-limit table all come from the same
resolved member the WebGL cluster comes from, so WebGL and WebGPU cannot
disagree -- the coherence is structural rather than maintained, and that is the
property not to break later by adding an independent WebGPU table.

Needs a build to verify. What is established without one is that it applies,
that the before-state was measured on the shipped artifact, and that the
admission predicate it extends is the only thing that admits a software adapter
on a GPU-less host.

Ledger: webgpu.adapter-info, webgpu.adapter-limits, webgpu.adapter-features.
